WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Plugin and extension systems are ABI-sensitive by nature.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:15.640
A host application and plugin must agree on initialization, shutdown, object ownership, version negotiation, error reporting, threading, and supported capabilities.

3
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:24.140
The plugin may be built by another team, vendor, or customer, so assumptions that are obvious to the host team may not be obvious to the plugin author.

4
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.460
A strict boundary reduces compatibility failures.

5
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.280
Avoid exposing internal objects directly.

6
00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:36.940
Prefer narrow exported functions, opaque handles, versioned contracts, and capability checks.

7
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:45.680
Define who owns host-provided objects, how long callbacks remain valid, which threads may call which functions, and how shutdown is coordinated.

8
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:52.900
A plugin contract should make it possible for an older plugin to fail gracefully when the host no longer supports its expectations.

9
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.660
Plugins may also be trust boundaries.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:04.560
Depending on product architecture, less-trusted modules may need process isolation, permission limits, validation, or defensive loading policy.

11
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:10.340
The goal is not to provide offensive guidance; it is to prevent compatibility failures and contain risk.

12
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.320
Treat plugin APIs as product commitments.

13
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:19.020
Document them carefully, test them across versions, and review lifecycle behavior before release.

14
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:25.840
If a plugin cannot meet the required contract, the host should fail safely and explain the incompatibility.

15
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.000
Good plugin systems make the first handshake explicit.

16
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:37.820
The host can ask for a declared ABI version, required capabilities, optional features, and shutdown behavior before accepting the module.

17
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:49.200
That negotiation gives the host a safe place to reject incompatible plugins, disable unsupported features, or choose a conservative compatibility path instead of relying on undefined behavior.
